; A lot whose annotation Ledger completes after reading its posting -- a
; valuation from its account's `value`, or the fixation of a lot bought
; with `@ =` -- is the same lot as one that a later posting writes with
; that annotation, so the two cancel in a balance: Assets holds only the
; $-120 paid, with no AAPL or MSFT left.
;
; A posting reaches a lot by naming its annotation, and the annotation
; Ledger completes names the lot as well: a later posting can name the
; valued AAPL lot with its valuation or without it, and `@ =` gives the
; purchase the fixated MSFT lot {=$7} [2024/01/03] itself, the lot that a
; posting writing that annotation names.  Were such a lot still found only
; by the annotation it was created with, a later posting that names its
; completed annotation would open a second lot with the same annotation,
; which a balance would keep apart from the first: Assets and the grand
; total would keep 10 and -10 of both AAPL and MSFT.

account Assets:B
    value $2

2024/01/01 Buy
    Assets:B    10 AAPL {$5} [2024/01/01]
    Assets:A    $-50

2024/01/02 Other
    Assets:C    -10 AAPL {$5} [2024/01/01] (($2))
    Equity

2024/01/03 Buy fixated
    Assets:F    10 MSFT @ =$7
    Assets:A

2024/01/04 Other fixated
    Assets:G    -10 MSFT {=$7} [2024/01/03]
    Equity

test bal --lots Assets
               $-120  Assets
               $-120    A
10 AAPL {$5} [2024/01/01] (({$2}))    B
-10 AAPL {$5} [2024/01/01] (({$2}))    C
10 MSFT {=$7} [2024/01/03]    F
-10 MSFT {=$7} [2024/01/03]    G
--------------------
               $-120
end test
